 we can said watching it  for you yesterday it was difficult yeah  it was difficult because again this is a  this isn't necessarily about the FBI and  it's not about the president this is  about the United States of America and  what we value and what is important to  us and and I think it's important to get  to the bottom of this  and I'm glad to see Bob Muller who I  dunno well is is going to be trying to  do it all right when you think about all  of this the Russian probe as you just  said it's a serious issue and it's  essential that we get to the bottom of  it and get all the facts out in bomb ala  has that challenge absolutely and again  I think nobody is better positioned to  do this than former director Bob Muller  one he understands how the process works  he is also a man of incredible integrity  and he will get to the bottom of it  speaking of integrity yeah which I know  you have have led your life with and  this book is not just about making your  bed you know I know that's the title but  it's the small things that can lead I  think to a life of value and integrity  where did you come up with those 10  things well of course it was part of the  speech as was raised in the in the clip  but the interesting thing about it was  as I was writing the speech about  Wednesday before I was scheduled to give  the speech on on Saturday I had writer's  block and the speech I was working on  just wasn't working and my wife god  bless her when I came to her and I'm a  little bit of a panic and she says look  write about something you know and I  said well I've been a Navy SEAL my whole  life  I don't know that students are going to  want to hear about being a Navy SEAL and  she said write about something you know  so I did I took seal training which  really is is kind of life in about six  months you learn everything I think you  need to learn in that six months you  learn how to fail you learn how to be  your best in your darkest moments you  learn the importance of making your bed  so I took those lessons from seal  training and put them into the speech  and then it's great Admiral we could say  that George Ann came through for you one  more time and she always does but one of  my favorite chapters is never quit  because in life always puts you in  situations where quitting will seem the  easy thing to do you said but quitting  is never easy and just when you think  you can't go on you really can well the  fact the matter is I'm convinced and I  think again it's one of the things you  learn in SEAL training is when you think  you have hit the wall when you think you  cannot go any further  the instructors show you that you can  they push you even harder and and the  folks that succeed the folks that make  it through SEAL training realize that  there's always something left in the  tank you can always continue on you say  if you want to change the world start  singing when  up to your neck and mud well and this is  in seal training in the middle of what  we referred to as hell week we go down  to the mud flats and at one point in  time we were all in there the  instructors were trying to get us to  quit and there were a bunch of kids were  getting ready to quit and and one  student started singing and the point  was it was a little bit about the power  of hope we realized that if he could  sing when he was up to his neck in mud  maybe we could sing too and we did and  before long we came together as a as a  group in the conversation I've had about  this that speech it puts his number of  times you said that it's not always the  people you expect to pass right now  they're very small few who passed the  test and get on become seals and they  don't look like and they're not always  the one when you look at the beginning  you would expect to pass well we had a  group you know again there's all sorts  of shapes and sizes come in the seal  training I think this is the misnomer  everybody expects all seals to be you  know six foot to 210 pounds bulging with  muscles and in fact most of the guys who  make it through are smaller they're wiry  but we had a group we referred to as the  Munchkin crew based on the pod there  were the small guys and and they were  remarkable they were some of the best  swimmers the best runners we had and you  learn very quickly that it really isn't  about as we used to say the size of your  flippers because they had small little  feet it wasn't the size your flippers it  was a size your heart that mattered  would you mind removing your shirt so we  can see is that a no okay let's go back  to the title of the book making your bed  because Nora said Gail did you make your  bed this morning no did you Charlie make  your bed this no no so I said good for  you Nora cuz she did in your book you  said Saddam Hussein didn't make his baby  but like this bad boy does not make you  a bad person I was asked that question  earlier just because you don't make your  bed does not make you Saddam Hussein is  even when you were injured when you  could recover the first thing you did  was make your bed I did and I think this  is you know it was instilled to me and  still to me at a young age it was  reinstalled in me when I got the SEAL  training kind of the importance of this  little task every morning I might get a  little task it helps you do the next  task and the next task and it also  reaffirms the fact that little things  matter but the most important thing you  say here don't quit don't know quickly  yeah never quit and and I think this is  true regardless if this isn't about SEAL  training this is about life I see great  students going through the University of  Texas System I will tell you they have  just the same amount of strain  and and and personal courage and  dedication to get through the world of  academia which is tough today thank you  so much for coming my pleasure thank you  admiral boorda and thank you and make  your bed is on sale now

